        "root_concepts": "[topological, spaces]",
        "section": "topology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Topological Spaces",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+====================================================================+\n|           TOPOLOGICAL SPACES — THE ABSTRACT FRAMEWORK              |\n+====================================================================+\n|                                                                    |\n|  TOPOLOGY τ on set X:                                             |\n|  Collection of subsets (called \"open\") satisfying:               |\n|  1. ∅ ∈ τ and X ∈ τ.                                             |\n|  2. Arbitrary union of opens is open: if Uα ∈ τ, then ∪Uα ∈ τ.  |\n|  3. Finite intersection of opens is open: U,V ∈ τ → U∩V ∈ τ.   |\n|                                                                    |\n|  TOPOLOGICAL SPACE: (X, τ).                                       |\n|                                                                    |\n|  WHY THESE AXIOMS?                                               |\n|  They are the MINIMAL axioms that make continuous functions work. |\n|  f: X→Y continuous ↔ f^{-1}(U) open in X for every open U in Y. |\n|                                                                    |\n|  KEY CONSTRUCTIONS:                                               |\n|  Subspace topology: on Y ⊆ X, opens = {Y∩U : U open in X}.      |\n|  Product topology: on ∏Xα, opens generated by ∏Uα.              |\n|  Quotient topology: on X/~, opens = sets whose preimage is open. |\n+====================================================================+\n```"
    },
