        "section": "topology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Topological Spaces",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The key insight: continuity is entirely open-set-theoretic (f continuous iff f^{-1}(open) = open), and the open set axioms are exactly what makes this work. The axiom restricting to *finite* intersections is load-bearing: ∩_n B(x,1/n) = {x} would force every singleton to be open, collapsing to the discrete topology. Infinite intersections destroy the structure."
    },
